metadata.vgl:
vgraph itsNotLuck: CRT "It's Not Luck — Current Reality Tree" {
    // ---------- Undesirable Effects ----------
    node ude_uniform: UndesirableEffect "Suppliers display a less and less uniform front.";
    node ude12: UndesirableEffect "(UDE #12) Salespeople are overloaded.";
    node ude1: UndesirableEffect "(UDE #1) Competition is fiercer than ever.";
    node ude9: UndesirableEffect "(UDE #9) In advanced material industries the constant introduction of new products confuses and spoils the market.";
    node ude2: UndesirableEffect "(UDE #2) There is an increasing pressure to reduce prices.";
    node ude8: UndesirableEffect "(UDE #8) In advanced material industries there is a need to launch new products at an unprecedented rate.";
    node ude_pressure_sales: UndesirableEffect "There is a pressure on salespeople to bring in more sales.";
    node ude_pressure_dev: UndesirableEffect "In advanced material industries there is unprecedented pressure to promptly develop new products.";
    node ude7: UndesirableEffect "(UDE #7) There is unprecedented pressure to take actions that will increase sales.";
    node ude_short_targets: UndesirableEffect "Companies are falling short of their stated financial targets.";
    node ude_not_improving: UndesirableEffect "Companies are not improving fast enough";
    node ude15: UndesirableEffect "(UDE #15) Companies don't come up with sufficient innovative ideas in marketing.";
    node ude10: UndesirableEffect "(UDE #10) Most new outlets and most new/improved products eat into the sales of existing outlets/products.";
    node ude13: UndesirableEffect "(UDE #13) Production and distribution do not improve fast/significantly enough.";
    node ude_marketing: UndesirableEffect "Marketing is not oriented to take advantage of the most promising and almost virgin direction - that of market segmentation.";
    node ude4: UndesirableEffect "(UDE #4) More than ever the market punishes suppliers who don't perform according to expectation.";
    node ude14: UndesirableEffect "(UDE #14) Engineering is unable to deliver new products fast and reliable enough.";
    node ude_drop_low: UndesirableEffect "Most companies are reluctant to accept orders with low margins and even go so far as dropping low-margin products.";
    node ude_trim_high: UndesirableEffect "Imposing a single price trims away customers for whom the price is too steep relative to their perception of value.";
    node ude_trim_low: UndesirableEffect "Imposing a single price enables customers who have a high perception of value to pay a low price.";
    node ude3: UndesirableEffect "(UDE #3) In more and more cases the price the market is willing to pay doesn't leave enough margin.";
    node ude_below_cost: UndesirableEffect "Most managers believe that selling below product cost leads (at least in the long run) to losses.";
    node ude_single_price: UndesirableEffect "Most managers believe that there is a single, fair price for a product.";
    node ude_cost_plus: UndesirableEffect "Most managers believe that the product price should be equal to product cost plus reasonable margin.";
    node ude_cost_real: UndesirableEffect "Most managers believe that product cost is something real that quantifies the efforts absorbed by the product.";
    node ude_local_meas: UndesirableEffect "There are important measurements that focus on local optima, e.g., cost accounting based measurements.";
    node ude_value_local: UndesirableEffect "Most managers' perception of value of a product is heavily influenced by the local efforts required to design, produce, sell and deliver the product.";
    node ude5: UndesirableEffect "(UDE #5) Managers are trying to run their companies by striving to achieve local optima.";
    node ude11: UndesirableEffect "(UDE #11) A large percentage of the existing sales force lacks sufficient sales skills.";

    // ---------- Intermediate Effects ----------
    node ie_diff_perceptions: IntermediateEffect "Different market sections might have different perceptions of value for even the same product.";
    node ie_value_key: IntermediateEffect "More than ever satisfying the market perception of value is key to success.";
    node ie_market_drives: IntermediateEffect "Prices and quantities sold are determined more and more by the markets' perception of value and less and less by the suppliers perception of value.";

    // ---------- Givens ----------
    node g_reduce_prices: Given "A desperate traditional method to get sales is to reduce prices.";
    node g_new_products: Given "One of the most effective ways to increase sales is to develop new, improved products.";
    node g_advanced_industries: Given "There are industries where fast development of new material enables the development of new products - advanced material industries.";
    node g_costs_cut: Given "Companies have already cut all costs they know how to cut.";
    node g_diff_needs: Given "Different market sections might have different needs.";
    node g_value_benefits: Given "Market perception of value is in accordance with the benefits of having the product.";
    node g_actions_dont_save: Given "Many actions that are needed for improving lead-time, reliability, quality, response time and service, do not save cost and/or increase cost in the short run.";
    node g_cost_accounting: Given "The essence of cost accounting is to calculate the product cost.";
    node g_measurements: Given "For every mode of operation, managers develop suitable measurements.";

    // ---------- Changeable ----------
    node c_segmentation: Changeable "Actions to guarantee effective segmentation can be taken";

    // ---------- Junctors ----------
    node and_competition: AndJunctor "";
    node and_pressure_sales: AndJunctor "";
    node and_pressure_dev: AndJunctor "";
    node and_value_key: AndJunctor "";
    node and_segmentation: AndJunctor "";
    node and_cost_plus: AndJunctor "";
    node and_local_optima: AndJunctor "";
    node and_market_drives: AndJunctor "";

    // ---------- Edges (root causes upward) ----------

    // Cost accounting / local optima chain
    edge g_cost_accounting -> ude_cost_real: given_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude_cost_real -> ude_value_local: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge g_actions_dont_save -> ude_value_local: given_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude_value_local -> ude_local_meas: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge g_measurements -> ude_local_meas: given_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude_local_meas -> ude5: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude5 -> ude_not_improving: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude5 -> ude13: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude5 -> ude14: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude5 -> ude11: undesirable_causes_undesirable;

    // Pricing chain
    edge ude_cost_real -> ude_cost_plus: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude_cost_plus -> ude_single_price: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude_single_price -> ude_trim_high: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude_single_price -> ude_trim_low: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude_trim_high -> ude3: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude_trim_low -> ude3: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude_below_cost -> ude_drop_low: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude_cost_plus -> ude_below_cost: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude3 -> ude2: undesirable_causes_undesirable;

    // Market perception chain
    edge g_diff_needs -> ie_diff_perceptions: given_causes_intermediate;
    edge g_value_benefits -> ie_market_drives: given_causes_intermediate;
    edge ie_diff_perceptions -> and_market_drives: intermediate_to_and_junctor;
    edge g_value_benefits -> and_market_drives: given_to_and_junctor;
    edge and_market_drives -> ie_market_drives: and_junctor_causes_intermediate;
    edge ie_market_drives -> ie_value_key: intermediate_causes_intermediate;
    edge ie_value_key -> ude4: intermediate_causes_undesirable;
    edge ie_value_key -> ude_marketing: intermediate_causes_undesirable;

    // Segmentation
    edge ie_diff_perceptions -> and_segmentation: intermediate_to_and_junctor;
    edge c_segmentation -> and_segmentation: changeable_to_and_junctor;
    edge and_segmentation -> ude15: and_junctor_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude15 -> ude_marketing: undesirable_causes_undesirable;

    // Competition / pressure to reduce prices
    edge ude4 -> ude1: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude_marketing -> ude1: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude1 -> and_competition: undesirable_to_and_junctor;
    edge g_reduce_prices -> and_competition: given_to_and_junctor;
    edge and_competition -> ude2: and_junctor_causes_undesirable;

    // New products pressure (advanced material industries)
    edge ude1 -> ude9: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge g_advanced_industries -> ude8: given_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude1 -> and_pressure_dev: undesirable_to_and_junctor;
    edge g_advanced_industries -> and_pressure_dev: given_to_and_junctor;
    edge and_pressure_dev -> ude_pressure_dev: and_junctor_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude_pressure_dev -> ude8: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude8 -> ude9: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude9 -> ude10: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude10 -> ude_uniform: undesirable_causes_undesirable;

    // Sales pressure
    edge ude_short_targets -> ude7: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge g_costs_cut -> ude_short_targets: given_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude7 -> and_pressure_sales: undesirable_to_and_junctor;
    edge g_new_products -> and_pressure_sales: given_to_and_junctor;
    edge and_pressure_sales -> ude_pressure_sales: and_junctor_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude_pressure_sales -> ude12: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude11 -> ude12: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude7 -> ude2: undesirable_causes_undesirable;

    // Falling short of targets
    edge ude2 -> ude_short_targets: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude_drop_low -> ude_short_targets: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude_not_improving -> ude_short_targets: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude13 -> ude_not_improving: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
    edge ude14 -> ude_not_improving: undesirable_causes_undesirable;
}
'''

When creating the tree from the book I realised a few things:

- Goldratt didn't fully define the tree in the book. I needed to make some connections up for myself. Please let me know if I went wrong. 
- Goldratt didn't connect UDE #11 at all. If you have read the book then you know that it was the task of Alex Rogo to connect all UDEs. 
- This seems to me to be the obvious example of CRT. However, I couldn't find anyone who had published this example. 

Fun fact: It took a while until I read [It's not Luck](https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/157385.It_s_Not_Luck). This is quite funny as I have used the [TOC Thinking Processes](/notations/TOC/index) for quite some time but I was simply not aware that they were presented in this book by Goldratt. Instead, I read [Thinking with Flying Logic](https://think.flyinglogic.com/lead-magnet1594533431870) a manuscript that is delivered together with the really good [Flying Logic](http://flyinglogic.com) software (which was a huge influence for Vithanco). Furthermore, I read Lisa Scheinkopf's [Thinking for a Change.](https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/732158.Thinking_for_a_Change) I even read [Dettmer's executive version book](https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/40027950-the-logical-thinking-process). 

Clearly, the book has a message and is therefore not a true novel and some of the examples are more questionable than others, but that is beside the point. I highly recommend the book.
